"""Easy Read versions of micro-skills 0-109 (src/entries.json micro[0:110])."""

EASY = {
    'micro::Choose a method suited to the referral question': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Pick the right way to watch and record a child in class.\n• Pick the way that fits the question the school asked.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Each way of recording gives a different kind of answer.\n• Some ways count how often something happens.\n• Some tell the story of what happened, but not how often.\n• The wrong choice answers a question nobody asked.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can say why you picked this way.\n• You can say what another way would have missed.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Referral question: the question the school or family wants answered.\n• Observation: watching a child carefully and writing down what you see.',
    'micro::Run a timed interval or event sample accurately': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Watch a child in short, equal blocks of time. Or count each time a behaviour happens.\n• Mark it on a grid as you go.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• If the time blocks are not equal, the numbers mean nothing.\n• If nobody else can read your grid, the record only lives in your memory.\n• Being steady and careful matters more than experience.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your time blocks are all the same length.\n• A colleague can read your grid.\n• You never fill in gaps from memory later.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Interval: a short, set block of time.\n• Grid: a sheet with boxes to mark what you see.',
    'micro::Record antecedent, behaviour and consequence separately': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Write down three things separately.\n• What happened before. What the child did. What happened after.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• This helps explain why a behaviour happens.\n• If you mix in your guess about the cause, readers cannot tell what you saw from what you decided.\n• That can cause harm. Others may repeat it for years.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You have not written the cause into what the child did.\n• Ask of each note: could a camera have recorded this?\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• ABC: Antecedent (what came before), Behaviour (what the child did), Consequence (what came after).\n• Behaviour: what a person does that you can see or hear.',
    'micro::Agree with the teacher how you will be introduced': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Agree with the teacher, before you arrive, how you will be introduced to the class.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• If the child knows you are there to watch them, they act differently. So does the class.\n• It is also about honesty. What the child is told must be true.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The child is told something true.\n• The child is not singled out.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Singled out: picked out from the others in a way everyone notices.\n• Observation: watching carefully and writing down what you see.',
    'micro::Position yourself to see without becoming the focus': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Choose where to sit in the classroom.\n• You need to see the child without the class watching you.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Where you sit changes what happens.\n• Sit too close and you become part of the lesson. Stand at the front and you look like an inspector.\n• Aim for a clear view, out of the way, and sit low.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The class settles down within a few minutes.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Line of sight: a clear view of what you came to see.',
    'micro::Observe without altering what you came to see': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Keep the change your presence makes as small as you can.\n• Notice that change, and write about it.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Being in the room always changes things a little. You cannot remove that.\n• A good report says how your presence may have changed things.\n• A general sentence that could fit any report is not enough.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can name how your presence may have changed things.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Reactivity: when people act differently because they know they are being watched.',
    'micro::Note peer interaction and social positioning': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Watch how the child gets on with other children.\n• Notice where they fit in the group.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Tests cannot show this. It is often what the worry is really about.\n• Teachers see the classroom. But much of what matters happens at break time.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can describe where this child fits among other children.\n• You give more than a vague impression like "seems to have few friends".\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Peers: children of about the same age, like classmates.',
    'micro::Note adult interaction and prompting patterns': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Watch how much help adults give the child.\n• Count the reminders and hints.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Adult help is often not written down anywhere.\n• It can be the most important thing in the room.\n• A child may seem to work alone but get reminders very often.\n• If nobody counts, the school may think the child can do more than they can.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can describe how much support the child was given.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Prompt: a reminder or hint from an adult to help the child start or keep going.',
    'micro::Observe the same child across two settings': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Watch the same child in two different places or lessons.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• One visit shows just one day in one room.\n• Seeing two places is a simple way to check.\n• Some diagnoses, like ADHD, need signs in two or more places.\n• If a child copes in one place and not another, the place matters too.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You do not make claims about the child from one lesson only.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• ADHD: Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder.\n• Diagnosis: a professional's decision that a person has a certain condition.\n• Setting: a place, like a classroom or the yard.",
    'micro::Keep description and interpretation separate': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Keep what you saw apart from what you think it means.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• This makes a report fair and easy to check.\n• Professional rules say opinion must be clearly marked as opinion.\n• If readers cannot tell the difference, they may repeat your guess as fact for years.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Opinion is labelled as opinion all through your report.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Interpretation: what you think something means.\n• Opinion: what you believe, not what you saw.',
    'micro::Link what you observed to the referral question': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Connect what you saw back to the question the school or family asked.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A long description that does not answer the question is no use to anyone.\n• The observation should help test an idea, weaken it, or show the question was wrong.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your observation answers something. It does not just describe everything.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Referral question: the question the school or family wants answered.\n• Hypothesis: an idea you are testing.',
    'micro::Prepare the history schedule and read it beforehand': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Get the list of questions ready.\n• Read it before you meet the parent.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• If you read questions for the first time in front of a parent, you look at the page, not at them.\n• The parent may lose trust in you.\n• Knowing the questions lets you leave the list when something important comes up.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You are not reading any question for the first time in the room.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• History: the story of the child's life and development so far.\n• Schedule: a list of questions to guide an interview.",
    'micro::Take pregnancy, birth and neonatal history': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Ask the parent about pregnancy, birth and the baby's first weeks.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Usually this rules things out.\n• Sometimes it explains a lot. For example, if the baby was born early or needed intensive care.\n• Ask gently, so the parent does not feel blamed.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You asked about problems without alarming the parent.\n• You did not skip it. You did not write down more detail than needed.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Neonatal: the first weeks after birth.\n• Intensive care: special hospital care for very sick babies.",
    'micro::Take early motor, speech and language milestones': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Ask when the child first walked, talked and reached other early steps.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The order of events matters most.\n• For example, did talking come late, before behaviour became hard?\n• Parents may not remember exact ages. How you ask helps them remember.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You have dates or rough ages, not just impressions.\n• You wrote what the parent actually said.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Milestones: key early steps, like first walking or first words.\n• Motor: to do with movement.',
    'micro::Ask about sleep, feeding and self-care': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Ask about sleep, eating, and how the child manages things like dressing and washing.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• These are the questions most often skipped. They can change the whole picture.\n• A child who sleeps very little may seem slow and unfocused on every test.\n• Eating and self-care show how the child copes day to day. They often slip first when a child is under strain.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You asked about all three.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Self-care: looking after yourself, like dressing, washing and using the toilet.\n• Adaptive functioning: how well a person manages everyday life.',
    'micro::Ask about play and peer relationships': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Ask parents about the child's play and friendships.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Play and friendship show how a child connects with others.\n• Parents see things school does not, like parties and time with cousins.\n• A child can have good language and still find friendships hard.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You asked about friendships, not only about language.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Social communication: how we use words and actions to connect with others.\n• Reciprocal: two-way, give and take.",
    'micro::Ask about medical history and medication': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Ask about the child's health and any medicine they take.\n• Ask about hearing and eyesight too.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The name of a medicine tells you little.\n• Ask what it is for, what it does, if it is taken regularly, and if the dose changed lately.\n• A recent change could explain the whole worry.\n• Hearing in the first school years is an important question that is often skipped.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You asked what effect a medicine has, not just its name.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Medication: medicine.\n• Dose: how much medicine is taken.",
    "micro::Open with the parent's own concern, in their words": 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Start by letting the parent say what worries them, in their own words.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• In the first minutes you hear how the parent sees things.\n• After many questions, they start to use your words instead.\n• Quoting parents accurately in the report helps them trust it.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The parent speaks first.\n• You have not steered them.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Concern: a worry.\n• Steer (lead): push someone towards a certain answer.',
    'micro::Ask about strengths before difficulties': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Ask about what the child is good at, before asking about difficulties.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• This is not just being polite.\n• Parents answer hard questions differently after talking about strengths. You get a fuller picture.\n• You also get real strengths to share at feedback, not made-up ones.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Strengths appear in your notes.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Strengths: things a child is good at or enjoys.\n• Feedback session: a meeting to share what the assessment found.',
    'micro::Follow up an ambiguous answer rather than accepting it': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• When an answer is vague, ask more about it.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Vague answers like "sometimes" or "he\'s grand" often hide useful information.\n• Passing them by is the most common reason a history ends up thin.\n• It is hard, because you must notice while listening, writing and thinking.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You noticed the vague answer at the time, and asked more.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Ambiguous: unclear, could mean more than one thing.\n• History: the story of the child\'s life and development so far.',
    'micro::Ask about family structure and significant events sensitively': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Ask about the family and big life events, gently and with care.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Events like a death, a separation, a move or illness can explain why things changed when they did.\n• Professional rules say only collect what you need for the purpose.\n• So get what you need, but no more.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You did not push when the parent hesitated.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Significant events: big changes in a family's life.\n• Formulation: your explanation of what is going on and why.",
    'micro::Manage emotional disclosure without losing the thread': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Respond kindly when a parent becomes upset.\n• Then carry on with the interview.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Parents often cry in these interviews. That is normal, not a failure.\n• Respond as a person. Do not brush past it or give up on the task.\n• If the parent talks about their own mental health, you listen and point them to help. You do not treat them.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You cared for the person and still finished the task.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Disclosure: when someone shares something personal.\n• Signposting: telling someone where they can get help.',
    'micro::Close with what happens next and when': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• End the meeting by saying what happens next, and when.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Parents often leave not knowing what will happen.\n• They wait, hear nothing, and feel forgotten.\n• A couple of minutes at the end prevents this.\n• Only promise what you can really do.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The parent leaves knowing the timeline.\n• You say more than "I\'ll be in touch".\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Timeline: when each next step will happen.',
    'micro::Place the child on the Continuum of Support': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Find out what level of support the child already has in school.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Irish schools use the Continuum of Support to decide who gets help.\n• If you suggest a level the child already has, the school will see you did not ask.\n• Knowing the level also tells you what has been tried and has not worked.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You know the child's level before you recommend anything.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Continuum of Support: the stages of help in Irish schools, from Classroom Support up to School Support Plus.\n• Recommend: suggest what should happen next.",
    'micro::Ask what has already been tried and what happened': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Ask what the school has already tried, and what happened.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Suggesting something the school tried last year loses their trust.\n• Ask how it was done, and for how long.\n• If help was given too little, "it did not work" tells you about how it was given, not about the child.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You did not assume nothing had been tried.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Intervention: a planned way of helping.',
    'micro::Ask about presentation across the timetable': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Ask if the difficulty shows up in all lessons, or only some.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A problem in every lesson is different from one only in English.\n• Changes by subject may point to the type of task.\n• Changes by teacher may point to relationships or teaching style.\n• Changes by time of day may point to tiredness, medicine or sleep.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You know if it changes by subject or by teacher.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Timetable: the plan of lessons in the school day.\n• Presentation: how a difficulty shows itself.',
    'micro::Clarify the referral question with the person who raised it': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Talk to the person who asked for help.\n• Be clear about their real question.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Referral forms are filled in quickly, often by someone else.\n• The question on the form may not be the real worry.\n• All your work aims at whichever question you accept.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can say the question in one sentence the person agrees with.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Referral: a request for a psychologist's help.\n• Referrer: the person who made the request.",
    'micro::Request standardised attainment data in advance': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Ask the school for its test results before you meet the child.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Schools already have group test results you can ask for.\n• Reading them first changes what you assess.\n• Sometimes they answer the question on their own. Then the child is spared a test they did not need.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You read the results before meeting the child.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Attainment: how well a child is doing in subjects like reading and maths.\n• Standardised test: a test given the same way to everyone and compared with many other children.',
    'micro::Ask who knows this child best in the school': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Ask who in the school knows the child best.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The person you meet is not always the one who knows most.\n• A support teacher, a special needs assistant, or last year's teacher may know more.\n• Asking takes one sentence. It often gives you better information.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You found the right person to talk to, not just the one who was free.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Informant: a person who gives you information about the child.",
    'micro::Agree what feedback the school gets and when': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Agree with the school, at the start, what feedback they will get and when.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• If this is not agreed, the school may chase you.\n• Or they may expect something you never planned to give.\n• Agreeing early helps you decide what the school really needs. That is often less than a full report, and sooner.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Expectations are set at the start.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Feedback: sharing what you found.\n• Expectations: what people think will happen.',
    'micro::Understand what each index measures': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Understand what each part of a thinking-skills test measures.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Parents will ask what a score means. A textbook definition will not help much.\n• Understanding also helps you read results correctly.\n• Verbal comprehension is about knowledge and reasoning with language, not just how many words a child knows.\n• Fluid reasoning is about solving new problems.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can explain verbal comprehension and fluid reasoning to a parent in plain words.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Index: a score for one group of skills in a test.\n• Cognitive: to do with thinking skills.',
    'micro::Know what the test does not measure': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Know what a thinking-skills test like the WISC-V cannot tell you.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• It does not measure school progress, motivation or creativity.\n• It does not show how the child copes in daily life.\n• Great care is needed if the child was tested in a second language.\n• Saying this stops a score being over-read for years.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can say what a WISC-V cannot tell you.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• WISC-V: Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children, fifth edition. It is a test of thinking skills.',
    'micro::Apply start points correctly for age and ability': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Start each test section at the right question for the child's age and ability.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The wrong start point spoils that section.\n• It can also spoil the bigger score it belongs to.\n• Start points differ by section and by age.\n• Sometimes you must judge where to start before you know much about the child.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You knew the rule without checking in the middle of the session.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Start point: the question where a test section begins.\n• Subtest: one section of a larger test.",
    'micro::Apply reverse rules without hesitating': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Go back to easier questions smoothly when a child struggles at the start.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Reverse rules let the child get credit for easier questions they would pass.\n• If you use them late or unevenly, you underrate the child.\n• If you hesitate, the child may sense they have failed. That affects the rest of the session.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You apply the rule without hesitating. Your supervisor watches for this.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Reverse rule: a test rule for going back to easier questions.\n• Supervisor: an experienced psychologist who guides your work.',
    'micro::Apply discontinue rules correctly': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Stop a test section when the rules say to stop.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• These rules protect the child from failing again and again.\n• They also stop lucky guesses from pushing the score up.\n• Stopping may feel unkind. But it is the kindest choice. The child does not sit through questions they cannot do.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You did not carry on past the stop point to be kind.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Discontinue rule: a test rule that tells you when to stop a section.',
    'micro::Query and prompt only where the manual permits': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Ask follow-up questions only where the test manual allows.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Too many follow-up questions push scores up.\n• Then the score cannot be compared fairly with other children.\n• This is the most common trainee mistake. It comes from wanting the child to do well.\n• Anything beyond the manual is help, not assessment.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You did not ask extra questions to help the child succeed.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Query: a follow-up question, like "tell me more".\n• Manual: the book of rules for a test.\n• Norms: scores from many children, used for comparison.',
    'micro::Prepare and check the kit before the child arrives': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Check that all the test materials are there before the child arrives.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A missing piece in the middle of a task can spoil that task.\n• The child may also lose confidence in you.\n• Kits get borrowed and pieces go missing. The only safe time to find out is before the child comes in.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Nothing is missing in the middle of a task.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Kit: the box of test materials.',
    'micro::Set up table and seating correctly': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Set up the table and chairs the right way for testing.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The child must see the picture book clearly.\n• You must see your record form, but the child must not.\n• Sitting across the table can feel like a face-off. Sitting side by side makes timing awkward.\n• Sitting at the corner usually works best.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The child sees the picture book.\n• You see the record form, and the child does not.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Stimulus book: the book of pictures and puzzles shown to the child.\n• Record form: the sheet where you write the child's answers.",
    'micro::Read instructions verbatim': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Read the test instructions exactly as written.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The test scores are based on those exact words. Changing the words changes the task.\n• This often happens when you are rushed, or think the child did not understand.\n• If a child really cannot follow the instruction, that is a finding. Do not fix it by rewording.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You did not change the words, even when time was short.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Verbatim: word for word.\n• Norms: scores from many children, used for comparison.',
    'micro::Time accurately while managing engagement': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Time the tasks correctly while also keeping the child involved.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Some tasks are timed, and the time is part of the score.\n• It is hard to time while you watch the child, write answers and manage behaviour.\n• If timing slips, the score cannot be compared with anything.\n• Never guess times and write them in afterwards.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your timing did not slip while you managed behaviour.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Engagement: staying interested and involved.',
    'micro::Record responses verbatim including errors': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Write down the child's answers word for word, including wrong answers.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• This lets someone else check and re-score the test.\n• Wrong answers can tell you a lot about how a child thinks.\n• You can only see this if you wrote down what they said.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Another psychologist could re-score from your record.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Verbatim: word for word.\n• Protocol: the written record of a test session.\n• Re-score: to mark a test again to check it.",
    'micro::Use the record form correctly including Q and P': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Fill in the record form fully.\n• Mark each follow-up question with Q, and each prompt with P.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The record form is the official record of the test.\n• Anyone re-scoring needs to see the Q and P marks.\n• If the form has gaps, nobody can check the score.\n• It is the first thing an examiner looks at.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The form is complete and easy to read.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Q: marks a query, a follow-up question.\n• P: marks a prompt, a set reminder.\n• Record form: the sheet where you write the child's answers.",
    'micro::Establish rapport before beginning': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Help the child feel at ease before testing begins.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Rapport is about getting true results, not just manners.\n• A worried child does less well on every part of the test.\n• Then the scores show their worry, not their ability.\n• A few minutes of warm-up is a small cost for a big gain.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The child looks at ease before the first task.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Rapport: a friendly, trusting connection.',
    'micro::Explain what will happen in language the child understands': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Tell the child what will happen, in words they understand.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A child who does not know what is happening worries instead of doing the task.\n• Children also have a right to know. Agreeing to take part needs understanding.\n• It helps a lot to say that some questions will be too hard for everyone.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The child could say it back to you, roughly.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Assent: a child's agreement to take part.",
    'micro::Notice fatigue, anxiety or disengagement': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Notice when a child is tired, worried or losing interest.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Children rarely say they have had enough.\n• They guess, go quiet, stop trying, or rush.\n• Noticing early keeps the results true. Otherwise the test just shows how long they could last.\n• What you notice about behaviour is often more useful to the school than the scores.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You noticed before the child told you.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Fatigue: tiredness.\n• Disengagement: losing interest and stopping trying.',
    'micro::Offer a break and record it at the time': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Offer the child a break.\n• Write it down at the time.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Breaks are allowed, and they help keep results true.\n• Where the break fell matters when you read the scores.\n• Writing it down at the time takes seconds. Trying to remember later is just a guess.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The break is noted on the record.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Protocol: the written record of a test session.\n• Validity: whether a result truly shows what it claims to.',
    'micro::Stop if the child indicates they want to': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Stop the test if the child shows they want to stop, even without words.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Professional rules say you must notice signs without words, not only words.\n• A child who has gone silent and stopped trying has taken back their agreement.\n• Talking them into carrying on breaks consent rules.\n• It also gives results you cannot trust.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You stopped, rather than talked the child into carrying on.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Consent: agreeing to take part.\n• Non-verbal: without words, like body language.',
    'micro::Close so the child leaves feeling competent': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• End the session so the child leaves feeling capable.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The child may be tested again, maybe many times.\n• How this session ends shapes how they feel next time.\n• It also matters for its own sake. The child has just answered many questions that were too hard, on purpose.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Look at how the child walks out.\n• You avoid false praise like "you did great".\n• You do not hint at results.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Competent: able to do things well.',
    'micro::Select subtests matched to the referral question': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Pick only the parts of a school-skills test that fit the question asked.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The WIAT-III has many parts. Giving all of them wastes time and tires the child.\n• Ask: which parts could change my answer?\n• Any part that cannot is not harmless. The child pays for it by getting tired.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can explain why you left out what you left out.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• WIAT-III: Wechsler Individual Achievement Test, third edition. It tests reading, writing and maths.\n• Subtest: one section of a larger test.',
    'micro::Administer to standard': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Give the school-skills test exactly by the rules.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The same rules as other tests apply.\n• In reading-aloud tasks you must mark mistakes live while timing. You cannot rebuild this later.\n• Writing tasks have exact words and times. Trainees often make these up.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Any slips you made would not change the overall score.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Standard: the set way to give a test.\n• Oral reading fluency: how smoothly and quickly a child reads out loud.',
    'micro::Score the protocol the same day': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Score the test on the same day you give it.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Memory fades fast.\n• Some tasks, like reading aloud and writing, depend on what you saw. A week later you are scoring your memory.\n• Unscored tests also pile up. They cause a rush at the end of the school year.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You do not let scoring pile up.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Score: to mark a test and work out the results.\n• Protocol: the written record of a test session.',
    'micro::Check every raw-to-scaled conversion': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Check every change from the raw score to the final score.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• One copying mistake can change a big score.\n• Nobody can see the mistake in the final report. It may be quoted for years.\n• Checking twice takes minutes. It is the easiest quality check you have.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You know one copying mistake can change a result, so you check every one.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Raw score: the number of points the child got.\n• Scaled score: the raw score changed so it can be compared with children the same age.',
    'micro::Apply confidence intervals rather than point scores': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Report a range of scores, not just a single number.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A score is an estimate, with some error around it.\n• A single number looks more exact than it is.\n• Then people treat a small change as meaningful.\n• A range is more honest. The ranges are in the test manual.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your report does not suggest false exactness.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Confidence interval: the range the true score is likely to fall in.',
    'micro::Compare attainment against cognitive profile appropriately': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Compare a child's school results with their thinking-skills results, with care.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• School results need something to compare against. The thinking-skills profile gives that.\n• But this method has real limits. It misses some children with lower thinking-skills scores.\n• That is why it is no longer used alone to decide who has a difficulty.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You know what a gap between the two does not mean.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Attainment: how well a child is doing in school subjects.\n• Cognitive profile: a picture of a child's thinking skills.\n• Discrepancy: a gap between two scores.",
    'micro::Notice a score you do not believe and check it': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Notice when a score does not fit what you know about the child.\n• Check it before you report it.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The two most likely reasons are a scoring mistake or a problem in how the test was given.\n• Both are yours to find.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You re-scored the test, rather than just reporting the odd score.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Re-score: to mark a test again to check it.',
    'micro::Identify where sources converge': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Find where different sources of information agree.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Agreement from different people, places and methods is the strongest evidence you can have.\n• But check the agreement is real.\n• A teacher and a parent may both say "attention" but mean different things. One means daydreaming. The other means leaving the seat.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You show agreement clearly. You do not just assume it.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Source: a person, test or observation that gives information.\n• Converge: to come together or agree.\n• Triangulation: checking something using more than one method.',
    'micro::Identify where sources diverge and say so': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Find where sources disagree, and say so in your report.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Disagreement is useful information.\n• A child may seem anxious at home and settled at school. That tells you about the setting.\n• Research shows different people often only partly agree about a child.\n• Hiding disagreement hides a useful finding. It also makes you seem more certain than you are.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You name the disagreement rather than smoothing it away.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Diverge: to differ or disagree.\n• Setting: a place, like home or school.',
    'micro::Judge which source to weight and why': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Decide which source of information counts most, and explain why.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Sources are not equal.\n• A test score has known reliability. One short observation may catch an unusual day.\n• A parent knows the child over years, but not in class.\n• You must weigh them. Write down how you did it, so others can question and check it.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your weighting is fair and written down.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Weighting: deciding how much each source counts.\n• Reliability: how steady and dependable a result is.',
    'micro::Write an integration section rather than a list': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Write a section that brings all the findings together, by theme.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A report that lists each test on its own leaves the hardest part to the reader.\n• Teachers and parents will not join it up themselves. They skip to the recommendations.\n• Report-writing books agree: organise by theme, not by test.\n• Reports without this are often sent back.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The reader does not have to join the findings up themselves.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Integration: bringing different findings together into one picture.\n• Recommendations: suggestions for what should happen next.',
    'micro::Ask whether the profile answers the referral question': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Check if your findings answer the question you were asked.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The referral question is like an agreement.\n• A good report that answers a different question has still failed the family and school.\n• It is easy to drift towards something interesting.\n• So reread the question and your conclusion. Check that one answers the other.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You notice when your report does not answer the question.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Referral question: the question the school or family wants answered.\n• Profile: the overall picture of the child from the assessment.',
    'micro::State what you did not assess and what that limits': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Say what you did not assess.\n• Say how that limits what you can conclude.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Every assessment covers only part of the picture.\n• If you do not say what was left out, readers think it was covered.\n• Professional rules say you must state the limits of your evidence.\n• Examiners and complaints look for this first.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your report has a clear paragraph about limits, written for this child.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Limitations: what your assessment cannot show.\n• Assessment: gathering information to understand a child's needs.",
    'micro::Explain why phonological awareness matters for reading': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Explain why hearing and playing with the sounds in words matters for reading.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Teachers may ask why you tested sound games when the worry was reading.\n• One well-known model splits reading into two parts: working out words, and understanding language.\n• Trouble with sounds is the best-supported reason why working out words fails.\n• If you cannot explain this plainly, your advice may not be used.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can explain it to a teacher without notes.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Phonological awareness: noticing and working with the sounds in words.\n• Decoding: working out a written word from its letters.',
    'micro::Distinguish a specific difficulty from a general one': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Tell if a reading difficulty stands on its own, or is part of a wider difficulty.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A child weak in reading and in general language needs different help.\n• A child whose reading stands apart from their other skills needs something else.\n• Current views see dyslexia as a range, not a gap between test scores.\n• You must still check the wider picture, especially spoken language.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You check the thinking-skills profile before you decide.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Dyslexia: a difficulty with learning to read and spell.\n• Specific difficulty: a difficulty in one area, while other skills are typical.\n• Oral language: spoken language.',
    'micro::Administer phonological awareness subtests to standard': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Give sound-based test tasks exactly by the rules.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• These tasks are spoken, so your voice is part of the test.\n• Speak too slowly, stress the wrong sound, or repeat something you should not, and the score shows your delivery.\n• Some tests use recordings for this reason. Check your manual.\n• Mistakes cannot be fixed at scoring.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You give the tasks smoothly, and your timing is accurate.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Phonological: to do with the sounds in words.\n• Manual: the book of rules for a test.',
    'micro::Administer rapid naming tasks accurately': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Give rapid naming tasks accurately.\n• The child names rows of colours, objects, letters or numbers as fast as they can.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• How fast a child names things helps predict reading speed. This is partly separate from sound skills.\n• The score is a time, often adjusted for mistakes.\n• A late start on the stopwatch, or a missed mistake, changes the result.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You timed it and recorded mistakes correctly.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Rapid naming: naming familiar things as quickly as possible.\n• Fluency: reading smoothly and at a good speed.',
    'micro::Score the phonological composites correctly': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Work out the overall sound-skills scores correctly.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Schools act on these overall scores.\n• They are built in several steps. Each step uses the child's exact age on the day.\n• Which tasks go into which score changes with age, so check the manual.\n• One wrong table changes the whole picture.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You worked out the overall scores and checked them.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Composite: an overall score made from several task scores.\n• Percentile: how a child compares with others the same age.",
    'micro::Interpret a phonological profile in context': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Understand what low sound-skills scores do and do not show.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A low score shows the child found these tasks hard, on that day, compared with others.\n• On its own it does not prove dyslexia, explain every reading problem, or show which help will work.\n• Look at it beside reading, spoken language, teaching history, and the child's own view.\n• Claim too much and the family gets a label the evidence does not support.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can say what the scores do and do not show.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Dyslexia: a difficulty with learning to read and spell.\n• Context: everything around the score that helps explain it.",
    'micro::Link the profile to what the school can deliver': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Make recommendations the school can really carry out.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Advice the school cannot deliver is not really advice.\n• What is possible depends on staff, timetable and training.\n• Advice should follow from what you found. For example, weak sound skills point to clear, step-by-step teaching of sounds and phonics.\n• Make it specific enough to act on.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your recommendation follows from what you found.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Phonics: teaching the link between letters and sounds.\n• Recommendation: a suggestion for what should happen next.',
    'micro::Recognise the limits of a screening instrument': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Know what a screening questionnaire can and cannot tell you.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A screen finds children who may need a fuller assessment. It does not diagnose.\n• Screens use one person\'s view. How accurate they are changes with age and group. Check the manual.\n• A family may hear "the score was high" as "your child is autistic". That misleads them.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You wrote clearly that a screen is not a diagnosis.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Screen: a quick check to see who may need more assessment.\n• Diagnosis: a professional\'s decision that a person has a certain condition.',
    'micro::Know the onward diagnostic pathway in this service': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Know where a family can go for a full autism assessment in your area.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A worrying screen with nowhere to go leaves a family stuck.\n• In Ireland, this is usually done by HSE teams, or privately.\n• The Assessment of Need is one legal route.\n• Routes and waiting times differ by area and change often. Find out what is true locally now.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can name where a positive screen goes next.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• HSE: Health Service Executive, which runs public health services in Ireland.\n• Assessment of Need: a legal route to have a child's needs assessed, under the Disability Act 2005.",
    'micro::Administer a screen with a parent': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Help a parent fill in a screening questionnaire about their child.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Parents may be worried, or want to protect their child.\n• Some may score high to get help. Some may score low to avoid a label.\n• Explain what the screen is for, what it cannot tell them, and what happens next.\n• This is part of consent. It also makes answers more accurate.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You explained what a screen can and cannot tell them.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Informed consent: agreeing after being told clearly what will happen.\n• Screen: a quick check to see who may need more assessment.',
    'micro::Integrate screening data with direct observation': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Put questionnaire results together with what you see yourself.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A questionnaire gives one person's summary.\n• Watching shows you the child in real places.\n• Each can mislead on its own.\n• Autism guidance asks for information from more than one source and setting. Check what your service's guidance says.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You did not rely on the questionnaire alone.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Observation: watching a child carefully and writing down what you see.\n• Setting: a place, like a classroom or the yard.",
    'micro::Recognise masked or atypical presentation': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Notice that some autistic children hide their differences.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Some autistic children, often but not only girls, learn to hide social differences. This costs them a lot.\n• This is called camouflaging.\n• Research suggests girls are often missed. Screens and short visits may miss them too.\n• Read the PSI autism guidelines yourself.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You thought about how autism can look in girls, and about children found late.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• PSI: Psychological Society of Ireland.\n• Camouflaging: hiding or covering up differences to fit in.',
    'micro::Select the right forms for informant and age': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Choose the right rating form for the person filling it in, and for the child's age.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Rating forms come in sets.\n• There are versions for parents, teachers and the young person, at different ages.\n• The wrong form compares the child with the wrong group.\n• Check the exact ages in the manual first.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You picked parent, teacher and self-report forms correctly.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Rating scale: a form where people rate how often behaviours happen.\n• Self-report: a form the young person fills in about themselves.",
    "micro::Include the child's own self-report where age-appropriate": "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Include the child's own view, where their age allows.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Children have a right to share their views on things that affect them. Those views must be taken seriously (UNCRC).\n• The child's account sits beside the adults', not below it.\n• Adults often miss worry and sadness, because they see only behaviour.\n• Check the child's view was heard and acted on.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The child's account appears alongside the adults'.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• UNCRC: United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child. Article 12 is about children's right to be heard.\n• Self-report: a form the young person fills in about themselves.",
    'micro::Explain to informants how to complete them': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Explain to parents and teachers how to fill in rating forms.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A form is only useful if it is filled in properly.\n• Forms come back with gaps, extra ticks, or notes instead of ratings.\n• Sometimes two parents fill it in together without saying.\n• A short explanation saves a wasted form.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The forms come back usable.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Informant: a person who gives you information about the child.\n• Rating scale: a form where people rate how often behaviours happen.',
    'micro::Score and convert to standardised scores': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Score rating forms and change them into standard scores.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Most forms turn raw scores into T-scores, compared with a group of similar children.\n• The wrong group, missed reversed questions, or typing errors all change the result.\n• Nothing on the page shows the mistake.\n• Checking is what makes the score trustworthy.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your scoring is checked.\n• You look at the form's own checks for odd answering.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• T-score: a standard score where 50 is average.\n• Validity check: a part of a form that flags odd answering patterns.",
    'micro::Analyse informant agreement and discrepancy': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Look at where parent, teacher and child ratings agree or disagree.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Ratings from different people often disagree.\n• Research found only modest agreement, especially between different types of people.\n• Disagreement often shows real differences in how the child acts in different places.\n• Treating it as someone being wrong throws that away.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You treat disagreement as information about the setting.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Informant: a person who gives you information about the child.\n• Discrepancy: a difference or gap.',
    'micro::Distinguish elevated from clinically significant': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Know the difference between a raised score and a "clinically significant" one.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Rating forms put scores into bands. Each band has a set meaning.\n• Different forms use different cut-offs, so check each manual.\n• "Clinically significant" means the score is rare compared with other children. It is not a diagnosis.\n• Misusing these words misleads readers about how worried to be.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You know what the cut-offs mean and say so.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Cut-off: the score where one band ends and another begins.\n• Clinically significant: a score that is unusual compared with other children.',
    'micro::Choose an adaptation proportionate to the need': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Change how you give a test only as much as the child needs.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Some children cannot manage a test as written. Their senses, movement, language, attention or worry get in the way.\n• Refusing to adapt can break equality law.\n• Adapting too much means the score no longer compares with other children.\n• Choose the smallest change that removes the barrier.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You chose a break, rewording, a new order or stopping on purpose.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Adaptation: a change to how a test is given.\n• Reasonable accommodation: a fair change so a person can take part.',
    'micro::State the effect on validity in the report': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Say in your report how any changes to the test affect the scores.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Scores compare the child with children tested in the standard way.\n• Once you change things, readers need to know if the comparison still holds.\n• Guidance says changes must be reported with their likely effect.\n• Otherwise readers think the score means the same as any other.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You said whether the scores can still be understood and trusted.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Validity: whether a result truly shows what it claims to.\n• Standard conditions: the set way a test is meant to be given.',
    'micro::Recognise when standard procedure is not working': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Notice early when the standard way of testing is not working for a child.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The child may lose interest, get upset, not understand, or answer from worry.\n• The later you notice, the more of the test is spoiled.\n• Early signs are small, like slower answers or a change in how they sit.\n• Noticing early means a small change can be enough.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You noticed early, not at the end.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Standard procedure: the set way of giving a test.',
    'micro::Record the adaptation at the moment it happens': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Write down any change to the test at the moment you make it.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• By the time you write up, you will not remember the details.\n• Professional rules link good records to being accurate and on time.\n• Notes made at the time are the only way to judge which scores were affected.\n• Without them you cannot write honestly about how far to trust the scores.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Changes are noted on the record at the time, not remembered later.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Protocol: the written record of a test session.\n• Validity: whether a result truly shows what it claims to.',
    'micro::Re-evaluate whether to continue at all': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Keep asking yourself if the session should go on at all.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Sometimes the right choice is to stop.\n• A child who is upset, unwell, or cannot do the task is not giving true results. Carrying on may harm them.\n• Agreement to take part can change at any time.\n• Stopping well is a professional choice, not a failure.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You were willing to stop.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Assent: a child's agreement to take part.",
    'micro::Read all four frameworks named in the handbook': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Read the four frameworks named in your course handbook.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The four are Monsen's model, COMOIRA, the Interactive Factors Framework, and Carr's model.\n• If you do not read them, you still use a framework. It is just a hidden one.\n• It is often one that sees the problem only inside the child.\n• And you cannot defend it when asked.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can say what each framework focuses on.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Framework: a set way of thinking through a case.\n• COMOIRA: Constructionist Model of Informed and Reasoned Action.",
    'micro::Identify what each framework is good for': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Know which kind of case each framework suits best.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Monsen's model suits questions people disagree about.\n• The Interactive Factors Framework suits learning and development needs at several levels.\n• COMOIRA suits cases where adults hold very different views.\n• Carr's model suits cases about how a problem started and what keeps it going.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can say which type of case suits which framework.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Framework: a set way of thinking through a case.\n• COMOIRA: Constructionist Model of Informed and Reasoned Action.",
    'micro::Identify what each leaves out': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Know what each framework can miss.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Every framework has a blind spot.\n• Monsen's model can tidy away ideas that were not settled.\n• The Interactive Factors Framework's arrows are easy to draw but hard to justify.\n• COMOIRA can drift from the evidence. Carr's model focuses on the child and can underplay the classroom.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can name one limit of the framework you like best.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Blind spot: something you tend not to see.\n• COMOIRA: Constructionist Model of Informed and Reasoned Action.",
    'micro::Make your preferred framework explicit in supervision': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Tell your supervisor which framework you tend to use.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Everyone has a default.\n• Saying yours out loud lets your supervisor watch for its blind spot.\n• They can challenge you when it does not fit the case.\n• A hidden habit cannot be corrected by either of you.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your supervisor knows what you default to.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Supervision: regular meetings where an experienced psychologist guides your work.\n• Default: what you use without thinking.',
    'micro::Ask your supervisor which they use and why': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Ask your supervisor which framework they use, and why.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Your supervisor's habits shape how they read and correct your reports.\n• Knowing if they were trained in a framework, or learned from experience, tells you what they look for.\n• It is a fair question. Most supervisors welcome it.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You know whether they were trained in it.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Supervisor: an experienced psychologist who guides your work.\n• Framework: a set way of thinking through a case.",
    'micro::Choose a framework deliberately for this case': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Choose a framework on purpose for each case.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The question should choose the framework, not habit.\n• Ask: is it disputed, at many levels, a disagreement between adults, or about how it started and continues?\n• Choose before you collect information. The framework decides what you need.\n• Choosing afterwards is just justifying. Examiners notice.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can say why you chose this framework and not another.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Framework: a set way of thinking through a case.\n• Referral question: the question the school or family wants answered.',
    'micro::Name and cite it in the report': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Name the framework in your report, and give its source.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Naming it tells the reader how to read your explanation. The source lets them check it.\n• It shows your thinking rests on a published model.\n• Using a framework without naming it hides its shape.\n• Naming one you did not really use is worse.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The framework appears by name, with a reference.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Cite: to name the source.\n• Formulation: your explanation of what is going on and why.',
    "micro::Use the framework's own structure": "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Let the framework you chose shape your report.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Test yourself: remove the framework's name. Could a reader still tell which one you used?\n• With Monsen's model, you show each idea with evidence for and against.\n• With Carr's model, you show what set things up, started them, keeps them going, and protects.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The framework's stages are clear to see, not just hinted at.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Framework: a set way of thinking through a case.\n• Formulation: your explanation of what is going on and why.",
    'micro::Show the reasoning step by step': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Show each step of your thinking in the report.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• A conclusion without steps cannot be checked. People can only accept it or reject it.\n• Professional rules say keep facts, opinions and ideas apart.\n• Show the scores, what was ruled out, and the simplest explanation left.\n• Then people can argue with it. That is the point.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Someone could point to the exact step where they disagree.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Conclusion: what you decide at the end.\n• Reasoning: the steps of thinking that lead to a conclusion.',
    "micro::Include the child's construction of the problem": "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Include the child's own view of the problem in your explanation.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• It is the part most often left out of trainee reports.\n• Children have a right to be heard.\n• Ask what they find hard, what helps, and what they would change.\n• If it differs from the adults' view, that is a finding.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The child's view shapes your explanation, not just decorates it.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Formulation: your explanation of what is going on and why.",
    'micro::Generate more than one hypothesis': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Come up with more than one possible explanation.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• The worry on the form is often not the main need.\n• A school may ask about behaviour, when it comes from a reading difficulty nobody spotted.\n• Aim for at least two real, competing ideas.\n• With only one idea, you may treat a symptom, and it will not work.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You really thought about another explanation.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Hypothesis: an idea you are testing.\n• Symptom: a sign of a problem, not the problem itself.',
    'micro::Record the hypothesis you rejected and why': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Write down the explanation you ruled out, and why.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• This shows you tested ideas rather than assumed them.\n• It is one of the clearest signs of a strong report.\n• If you leave it out, others cannot see what was ruled out.\n• They may repeat the same work, or think it was never considered.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• The idea you rejected appears in your report.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Hypothesis: an idea you are testing.\n• Rejected: ruled out after checking.',
    'micro::Test each hypothesis against the data': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Check each explanation against all your information.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Two useful tests are timing and spread.\n• Timing: what came first? Spread: does the problem show up wherever the demand is, or only in one place?\n• Looking only for evidence that agrees is a classic mistake.\n• So is thinking that two things together means one caused the other.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You can say what supports each idea, and what goes against it.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Hypothesis: an idea you are testing.\n• Evidence: information that helps show whether something is true.',
    'micro::State explicitly which need is primary': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Say clearly which need is the main one.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• This is one of the most valuable steps in your explanation.\n• If you only explain the surface problem, you recommend help for a symptom.\n• Listing two difficulties side by side leaves readers to guess which comes first.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your report says which need is main, not just lists both.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Primary need: the main need that drives other difficulties.\n• Secondary need: a difficulty that comes from the main need.',
    'micro::Recognise where the sequence is genuinely unclear': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Say honestly when you cannot tell which need came first.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Sometimes records are missing, the child moved school, or people disagree.\n• Forcing a neat answer is a known mistake.\n• A confident guess may lead to the wrong help first.\n• "We cannot tell yet, and here is how we will find out" can be updated later.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You said so, rather than just picking one.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Sequence: the order things happened in.',
    'micro::Know the common co-occurrences in this age band': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Know which difficulties often happen together in children of this age.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Having more than one difficulty is common, not rare.\n• Examples: ADHD with a learning difficulty, and DLD with a reading difficulty.\n• Others: DCD with ADHD and DLD, and autism with ADHD, anxiety or intellectual disability.\n• Numbers differ a lot between studies. Check a current source before quoting any.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You know common pairs, like a learning difficulty with ADHD, or anxiety hiding autism.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• ADHD: Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder.\n• DLD: Developmental Language Disorder.\n• DCD: Developmental Coordination Disorder.',
    'micro::Consider twice-exceptionality': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Think about whether a child may be very able and also have a difficulty.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• High ability can hide a difficulty.\n• The child may get average results while working much harder than others.\n• School checks look at results, so these children are often missed.\n• Look for big differences between thinking skills, not just the overall score.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You thought about high ability hiding a difficulty.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Twice-exceptional: very able and also having a difficulty.',
    'micro::Consider a second presentation as routine': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Always think about whether a child may have a second difficulty.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Once a child has one diagnosis, other difficulties get blamed on it.\n• This is the most common way children get missed.\n• Before you start, write down what the first diagnosis does not explain.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You think about a second difficulty every time, not only when it is obvious.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Diagnostic overshadowing: when one diagnosis hides another need.\n• Diagnosis: a professional's decision that a person has a certain condition.",
    'micro::Weigh the evidence for each separately': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Look at the evidence for each difficulty on its own.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• For each one, ask: what would show it, what is there, and what is missing?\n• If one diagnosis explains everything, a second need gets no help.\n• If you count the same sign for both, you may find too much.\n• Either mistake changes your advice.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• One diagnosis did not explain everything.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Evidence: information that helps show whether something is true.\n• Diagnosis: a professional's decision that a person has a certain condition.",
    'micro::State what would establish the second': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• When you think there may be a second difficulty, say what would be needed to confirm it.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Naming a worry without a next step leaves the family stuck.\n• As a trainee you do not diagnose ADHD, autism or medical conditions.\n• You describe what you see, say what it fits with, and refer on.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You named the next step.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• ADHD: Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder.\n• Refer: to pass someone on to another service.',
    'micro::Identify risk factors across all three levels': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Look for risk factors at three levels: the child, the family, and the school or community.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Examples are a developmental difficulty, illness in the family, or many teacher changes.\n• If you look at only one level, you end up seeing the problem only in the child.\n• Thinking about what set up, started and keeps a problem going makes risk useful.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You name risk as risk, not just as background.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Risk factor: something that makes difficulties more likely.',
    'micro::Identify protective factors genuinely present': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Find protective factors that are really there.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Base it on evidence, not hope.\n• "Supportive family" only counts if you can say what the family does.\n• Resilience often comes from ordinary things, like relationships, skills and belonging.\n• General strengths cannot be built on.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Strengths are specific, not general.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Protective factor: something that helps a child cope.\n• Resilience: being able to cope and recover when things are hard.',
    'micro::Recognise when risk crosses a safeguarding threshold': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Know when a risk becomes a child protection concern that you must report.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• At this point it is not just part of your explanation any more.\n• Under the Children First Act 2015, a mandated person must tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly.\n• Telling the school's safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\n• Carrying on after a disclosure, or only writing the worry in a report, is the wrong response.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You reported the concern, rather than just describing it.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Mandated person: someone the law says must report child protection concerns.\n• Safeguarding: keeping children safe from harm.\n• Disclosure: when a child tells you something worrying.",
    'micro::Build recommendations on a protective factor': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Build at least one recommendation on a strength the child already has.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Strengths are what help is built from.\n• Help that uses a strength is the kind a school keeps going.\n• For example, if a child reads happily with one teacher, use that teacher or her approach.\n• A strengths list not linked to recommendations is a known mistake.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• At least one recommendation uses a strength.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Protective factor: something that helps a child cope.\n• Recommendation: a suggestion for what should happen next.',
    'micro::Name which model of disability you are working from': 'WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Say which way of thinking about disability you are using.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Your model shapes your advice, even if you do not name it.\n• A medical model sees the difficulty in the child. A social model sees it in barriers around the child.\n• A biopsychosocial model holds both.\n• Most trainee reports use a medical model without meaning to, because the tests focus on the child.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• Your model is stated, and you stick to it.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Model of disability: a way of thinking about where disability comes from.\n• Biopsychosocial: looking at body, mind and surroundings together.',
    'micro::Consider biological and developmental contributors': "WHAT YOU LEARN TO DO\n• Think about how health and early development may add to a child's difficulties.\nWHY IT MATTERS\n• Birth, hearing, eyesight, sleep, illness, medicine and genetic conditions can all play a part.\n• For example, glue ear in the first school years may help explain a reading difficulty later.\n• This is often missed.\n• If you record history but do not use it, something treatable may be overlooked.\nHOW YOU KNOW YOU CAN DO IT\n• You use the history, not just record it.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Glue ear: fluid in the ear that can affect hearing for a while.\n• Developmental history: the story of a child's growth and early steps.",
}
